{{- /*
  users.acl — the ACL file referenced by the flagfile. One line per user:

    user <name> <state> #<sha256 of the password> <permission rules>

  Passwords are written as SHA-256 hashes, never in plaintext, and the line order is stable
  across runs (the users map is iterated in sorted order).

  Two system accounts come first: `default`, which applications connect as, and the
  administrative one, which exists only when a password was given for it. Everything the caller
  declared in `users` follows.

  DO NOT ADD COMMENT LINES HERE. The hash marker is a hash, so a line starting with one is read
  as a malformed user and Dragonfly rejects the WHOLE file — see below for what that costs.
  Blank lines are fine.

  The `default` user is always here and always enabled, because of how Dragonfly treats this
  file. Three behaviours make that the only safe shape, and all three fail QUIETLY — the file is
  dropped with a single line in the log, the service starts, and `default` is left as the
  built-in passwordless account with full access:

    - with an ACL file configured, Dragonfly takes its entire user list from it. If `default`
      is not named here, no password given elsewhere closes it.
    - a `default` line that disables the user (state off) voids the whole file.
    - so does any line the parser does not understand, a comment among them.

  So `default` carries a password and its own permission rules, and the unit refuses to render at
  all when no password was given and the caller has not asked for an open instance.
*/ -}}
user default on #{{ .input.password | sha256sum }} {{ .input.default_perms }}
{{- if .vars.admin_account_enabled }}
user {{ .input.admin_user }} on #{{ .input.admin_password | sha256sum }} {{ .vars.admin_perms }}
{{- end }}
{{- range $name, $u := .input.users }}
user {{ $name }} {{ $u.state }} #{{ $u.password | sha256sum }} {{ $u.perms }}
{{- end }}
